//
// PROBE: the value-building words the corpus never spells.
//
// `fold`, `fold_stack` and `lambda*` all drain a stack into one value, and
// `lambda!` and `make.call` convert one value into another. None appears in a
// corpus program, so this is the only place their shapes are pinned.
//
// The one worth reading twice is **`fold` reverses**: it drains the stack
// top-down and then reverses, so the list reads in the order the values were
// pushed rather than the order they came off. `1 2 3 fold` is `[ 1 2 3 ]`, not
// `[ 3 2 1 ]`.
//
"probe: fold drains the stack into a list, in push order" println
1 2 3 fold println
"probe: lambda! turns a list into a lambda" println
[ 1 2 ] lambda! type println drop
"probe: make.call turns a string into a CALL" println
"dup" make.call type println drop
"probe: match is an alias for valuemap" println
match type println drop
"probe: nodata and its aliases" println
nodata type println drop
∅ type println drop
"probe: comparison spellings" println
1 2 <= println
2 1 >= println
